<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=sled">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sled</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sledge, Sleigh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A sled, sledge or sleigh is a vehicle that slides across a surface, usually of ice or snow, built with a smooth underside or a body supported by runners that reduce friction. Evidence of sledges or skids used for transport has been dated to before 10,000 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, by 3,200 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, and in Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Sleds and sledges were found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship">Oseberg ship</a> excavation, and a sledge was prized because, unlike wheeled vehicles, it was exempt from tolls. Closed winter sleds called vozoks carried the royalty, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and boyars of Muscovy, and man-hauled sledges were the tradition of British polar expeditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sled.jpg" alt="Sled" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pieter Delicaat · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sleigh_from_dutch_slee.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
